SM(590,{"n":"Hebrews 1.5-14","a":"Fr Stephen De Young Archives/Hebrews 1.5-14.mp3","d":2797.6,"s":[[0.16,"Come and study holy scriptures with us as Father Stephen DeYoung teaches verse by verse on the podcast The Whole Counsel of God."],[8.08,"Father Stephen is a former Protestant pastor and Bible teacher in the Reformed Church, but now an Orthodox priest, serving at Archangel Gabriel Orthodox Church in Lafayette, Louisiana."],[22.08,"Verse 5. To which of the angels did he ever say, You are my son, today I have begotten you."],[32.64,"That's a quote from Psalm two."],[36.8,"And Psalm Two was um one of the Psalms that was used at uh the enthronement and crowning of the King of Israel."],[54.4,"And that was the announcement."],[56.16,"Today you are my son."],[59.2,"Today I have begotten you."],[61.48,"Why? Well, the king in of Israel in the Old Testament was seen to be the Son of God in a certain sense."],[74.68,"What's going on in this section we're starting here, which is going to go on for a while, is we're disambiguating to use a nice modern internet term."],[88.2,"We're disambiguating son of God. of God or sons is used a whole bunch of different ways in the Old Testament"],[101.28,"So sons of God is used to refer to a particular high rank of angels at several points."],[110.16,"So you could see already, as we start this section, the transition, this is disambiguating."],[116.08,"When I say this is the son."],[117.86,"When we're talking about Christ as a son of God, we're not saying he's part of that high rank of angels."],[124.82,"We've got to disambiguate that."],[127.14,"He's the son in a different sense than their sons of God in a sense."],[137.24,"And yes, he is the Christ, he is the Messiah."],[142.09,"But he's also more than that."],[144.51,"He is the Son of God in a way that David wasn't."],[147.39,"David. That pointed to, but wasn't himself"],[157.02,"So the king was supposed to be the son of God in the sense of being God's image on earth."],[167.26,"Adam, in believe it's St."],[171.26,"Luke's genealogy, rather than St."],[173.5,"Matthew's. I'm pretty sure I'm right."],[177.5,"Is referred to as the Son of God."],[180.38,"The genealogy of Christ by St."],[183.18,"Luke. For the same reason."],[185.95,"He's made in the image of God."],[188.44,"So this is basic."],[189.32,"The Son is the image of the Father."],[190.56,"That's not just like normally through what we understand as genetics, your kid kind of looks like you."],[199.33,"Like that's that's true, but that's right."],[202.69,"But it goes beyond that."],[204.79,"It goes beyond that."],[206.63,"Where uh it's not just your sort of physical appearance that you pick up that a son picks up from his father."],[214.95,"Uh he learns all kinds of things from his father and picks up all kinds of attributes."],[220.57,"And character traits and things that are modeled to him, both through nature and through nurture."],[227.9,"And that also was seen to establish, it's not just image. of that physical, it's also established an ongoing relationship"],[237.11,"So that a son can, by the way he lives his life and what he does, bring honor to his father or dishonor to his father."],[245.35,"This is what's behind honor your father and your mother in the Ten Commandments."],[249.67,"It's not do whatever your parents tell you, even though that's how parents always interpret that commandment."],[256.31,"That's not actually what it means."],[257.67,"What it means is you live your life in a way that brings honor to your father and your mother."],[262.47,"Which sometimes, if your father and your mother are behaving in sinful ways, means not doing what they tell you to do."],[270.55,"Being better. Than they've taught you to be and want you to be."],[274.97,"Um, so that people see what the the child is doing and they say, like was said to Christ."],[283.25,"Blessed is the womb that bore you."],[285.86,"To bring to bring honor to them."],[287.62,"And so there's this relationship, and that's kind of an unbreakable relationship."],[291.86,"Even if the father disowns his son, then that itself will be a public scandal."],[296.84,"That that relationship is always sort of there."],[299.82,"De facto. And so that's a big part of how uh the image of God is understood in the Old Testament."],[307.44,"So Adam wasn't just when it says Adam's made of the image of God, it doesn't mean just like he physically resembled God."],[314.46,"It means that he was called. to image God, in the world"],[323.07,"And so the king is proclaimed to be the Son of God because he is called to do the same thing."],[330.91,"The king of Israel is called, and the kings of Judah later also is called to bring about God's justice, in the world, to show the compassion of God in the world, to show the leadership of God in the world."],[348.87,"To teach the commandments of God, to shape and guide and lead, the nation in the way God would have them go."],[357.31,"And to in always conduct himself in a way that brings honor to God, not just to himself."],[360.03,"or to And definitely never to behave in a way that would bring dishon the God of Israel."],[371.31,"So he is the king was the image of God in that sense and is the son of God in that sense."],[381.47,"So that's why this was used when he's being crowned as king."],[386.03,"This is a reminder."],[388.59,"You are now God's son."],[390.11,"This is a statement of responsibility."],[393.41,"Being the son, it's not just like, hey, you got adopted, and he's got a really cool house."],[399.74,"It's you've been adopted, so now you have this high responsibility."],[406.51,"Um, but so it's being cited here in terms of disambiguating from the angels."],[416.59,"Because none of the angels were told that."],[420.53,"That's not the role any of the angels had."],[425.1,"Certain humans have had it, but angels haven't."],[430.86,"And again, I will be to him a father, and he shall be to me a son."],[442.38,"That quote is from 2 Samuel, and that's God speaking through Nathan the prophet to David about his descendant or descendants."],[459.46,"The word seed in Hebrew, Zerah, which usually gets translated descendant or descendants in English, is in Hebrew a collective noun."],[471.3,"Like deer or fish. in my yard."],[478.02,"Or you could say there's one deer in my yard."],[481.54,"I own twelve fish, I own one fish."],[484.58,"Um Zorah is the same way"],[488.76,"And the Hebrew frequently plays with that."],[493.08,"Plays with that in terms of Ape the promises to Abraham."],[496.6,"Sometimes it's very clearly plural, sometimes it's pretty clearly singular."],[501.5,"And the same thing with David."],[503.6,"And they understood this."],[505.44,"This is why they were looking for a Messiah."],[508.34,"So on one hand, God's making a promise to David and his descendants."],[511.79,"The kings of Judah who would come after him."],[514.75,"But he's also making a promise about a very particular descendant."],[518.88,"The Messiah. The anointed one, the king who's gonna come out of that line down the road."],[525.32,"So both of these quotes here have to do with the Davidic king in in parti particular.cular, or in general, and the Messiah"],[536.73,"So the point here is that what makes Christ as Son of God different from the angelic sons of God is that he's also a human king, which none of the angels are."],[556.09,"So he's not an angel."],[560.61,"Because angels aren't humans."],[564.05,"But we've already been told essentially that he's God."],[572.58,"So again, this whole idea that. Christ is divine and human"],[578.38,"It's not something somebody came up with the second or third or fourth fifth century."],[585.28,"It's right here in the first, what, six verses of Hebrews."],[589.3,"God created all the ages through him."],[591.68,"For him. All this."],[592.89,"And then, oh, look, here's how he's different than the angelic sons of God."],[596.25,"He's also a human king descended from David."],[601.21,"And remember, we're still within this frame of understanding Psalm 110 109, verse 1."],[608.09,"The Lord said to my Lord, sit at my right hand until I make your enemies your footstool."],[612.57,"Remember the question Christ asked the scribes about that verse."],[619.33,"Christ quoted it himself, and he said, If the Messiah is David's son, why does he call him his Lord?"],[631.07,"Because the Father's greater than the Son."],[632.75,"We've just been talking about that."],[634.83,"So why would David call his descendant his Lord?"],[642.61,"So what we're reading here is directly related to that question."],[648.79,"Because the Messiah isn't just a human descendant."],[653.51,"That's what Christ was pointing to, and that's what's that's what we're seeing getting elaborated here."],[664.83,"Verse 6, but when he again brings the firstborn into the world, he says, Let all the angels of God worship him."],[676.91,"Um far do we want to go down this rabbit trail?"],[681.47,"Um, so this is a quote from Deuteronomy chapter 32."],[687.79,"Uh Deuteronomy chapter 32 is at least one of, if not the oldest piece of the Old Testament."],[704.79,"In in unaltered, unaltered form."],[707.97,"There may be a bunch of other things in the Torah that are as old, but they've been uh they've been edited and updated since."],[716.18,"And translated. The only other thing that vis with Deuteronomy 32 for age in the Torah is Exodus 15, the Song of the Sea."],[727.46,"After they cross the Red Sea."],[729.94,"Why do I say that?"],[731.22,"Well, because frankly, the Hebrew is almost impossible to translate."],[736.66,"Because it's not really Hebrew."],[739.62,"It's basically Paleo Hebrew."],[741.62,"It's something older than Hebrew."],[744.18,"It's like a dialect of old Canaanite."],[746.98,"Like. And so. I won't go, I I promise I won't go too far down this rabbit trail because we've talked about it before a bunch of times"],[757.98,"But if we say Moses wrote the Torah, and what we mean is the Torah as we have it today, that's not technically correct."],[773.47,"This is why traditional scholars today."],[778.77,"Conservative scholars today, don't talk about the mosaic authorship of the Pedateuch or the Torah."],[784.45,"They talk about the mosaic origin of the Torah."],[787.73,"Because the language that the oldest form of the Torah we have today is written in didn't exist when Moses was alive."],[794.87,"So Moses wrote some stuff."],[798.04,"And that stuff becomes the Torah as we have it now."],[804.61,"But it gets translated, it gets edited, it gets updated."],[810.21,"There are places where those updates are obvious."],[812.29,"Like it'll say it'll name a city and it'll say which used to be called this."],[816.35,"And that kind of thing."],[817.41,"Where somebody has come in and the language has been updated."],[821.19,"And this shouldn't give us the Hebie jeebies."],[824.04,"Because consider that, you know, we're in the Orthodox Church, where uh we pretty much always accepted the Greek translation that was made in the third century BC as being equally. authoritative as any of the Hebrew versions we have"],[840.8,"So if a third century translation is fine."],[843.77,"Nobody thinks Moses wrote in Greek."],[845.63,"So it shouldn't bother us so much that the Hebrew is also basically a translation."],[851.37,"But texts like Deuteronomy 32 and uh Exodus 15 have not been translated or updated."],[861.69,"They're still in this kind of archaic paleo-Hebrew thing."],[868.15,"And so you get lots of weird translations."],[873.61,"That those are probably the texts where if you look them up, you can do this online now if you go to Bibleate Gway or something, or Bible Hub, and pull up like 12 different translations of Exodus 15 or of Deuteronomy 32 that we're talking about now."],[888.61,"Like you'll be like, are they translating the same thing?"],[892.93,"And in some cases they're not."],[895.41,"That's um because different translators will try to figure out different things to do with it."],[901.57,"Um what we now call the quote unquote original Hebrew is based on manuscripts that start around the 11th century AD."],[914.69,"And that's a lot of times called the Masoretic text."],[918.53,"It'll be abbreviated MT for that."],[921.49,"And the reason it's called that is the Masor.etes were a particular school of Jewish scribes"],[929.45,"And what they did was, in addition to copying the text, they uh put in the little marks for the vowels."],[938.42,"Because Hebrew, like most Semitic languages, you don't write the vowels."],[943.14,"But they put in vowel marks because how you vocalize the word, what vowels you pronounce when you say it, can change the meaning of the word a lot in a lot of cases."],[954.68,"If you know some Arabic, you know exactly what I'm talking about."],[960.84,"The uh um, but so they went in and put in the the vowel marks, but that means they had to do an interpretation when they did that."],[968.28,"They were interpreting it a certain way by the way they put those marks"],[973.02,"And if you take the marks away you could read it very differently."],[976.92,"They also put in all kinds of margin notes along the sides of the bottom."],[982.2,"If you get a Hebrew uh Hebrew Bible or Hebrew Old Testament, you'll see there's all these margin they're all in Hebrew like all around the sides and the bottom and everything."],[993.0,"Um where they're commenting on the text and commenting on why they put the vowel marks they did and other things."],[1000.28,"If they noticed what they thought was a mistake, they wouldn't correct it."],[1004.76,"They would just copy exactly what they had."],[1007.28,"And then basically write in the margin, we think this is a mistake."],[1010.12,"We think this should say this other thing."],[1012.12,"But they still preserve what they have"],[1016.33,"This is in contrast to Christian copyists in the New Testament who are constantly trying to fix things."],[1023.37,"So there's we have lots of, but fortunately, we have thousands and thousands of manuscripts of the New Testament, so we can see all the places where they tried to fix things and we can correct for it."],[1033.37,"But it's a different approach."],[1036.01,"But anyway. So a lot of, especially older Bibles, older uh English Bibles will be translated from that Hebrew."],[1049.01,"Um newer ones in Deuteronomy 32 will take into account the Dead Sea Scrolls and uh 4Q Dute as it's abbreviated, which is the Deuteronomy Scroll they found in the fourth cave at Qumran in the Dead Sea Scrolls."],[1066.53,"Um which um nobody tried to fix."],[1072.85,"Um and it generally kind of matches uh the Greek translation better."],[1083.57,"Um because the uh the Septuagint, the old Greek translation from the third century BC of the Torah, uh tried to do something with it."],[1097.25,"Because again, they the Hebrew was no more that Paleo Hebrew was no more clear to them than it is to us."],[1103.61,"And so they were looking at it going"],[1108.17,"So they interpreted it, but um when we found the Hebrew of the Dead Sea Scrolls, which is like a thousand plus years older, uh we kind of it it it made sense of a lot of what was going on in the Greek."],[1125.69,"It was like, oh, okay, that's why they did that."],[1128.51,"Oh, I get it now."],[1130.01,"Um so some now Old Testaments you get in Deuteronomy 32 will take the Dead Sea Scrolls into account."],[1137.53,"Some of them will just kind of throw up their hands and just translate the Greek."],[1142.17,"Um because the Greek is at least clear. Choices"],[1144.25,"I mean,' in the Greek and just said, this is what were going with."],[1150.51,"Yeah, so the quote here follows the Greek."],[1164.67,"Now, who's he talking about?"],[1170.51,"Deuteronomy 32. Who is who is being referred to as the firstborn coming into the world?"],[1180.59,"Adam. Yeah, that's Adam."],[1188.19,"Let all the angels of God. worship him"],[1193.11,"You go, wait, what?"],[1198.16,"Um, and this is uh this reading from Deuteronomy 32 gets elaborated in tandem with uh the fall of the devil."],[1215.12,"When you read uh pseudepigraphic literature, which is when you read Seondc Temple Jewish literature."],[1221.49,"Jewish literature from the Second Temple period from 6th century BC to 1st century AD."],[1228.21,"Be becausecause you know, uh most of this literature is written to like answer questions and to try to interpret and connect dots and figure things out."],[1236.38,"So one of the things is to try to figure out, well, why does the devil"],[1240.2,"They saw the devil falling in Genesis 3."],[1243.96,"Why does he go after Adam and Eve?"],[1246.94,"And he ends up getting cursed."],[1249.08,"And falling. So why?"],[1254.8,"And they brought in this verse."],[1259.06,"Now, worship here is being loose, used loosely."],[1261.78,"It really is more bow before."],[1265.4,"Bow to. Bow to it."],[1268.76,"So the idea here, when you read that larger literature, is that uh, and this actually. gets picked up in Islam of all things"],[1276.64,"Um, but um uh in this literature, the idea is that man is made in the image of God."],[1285.02,"And they're picking up on this idea that what you have going on in in Genesis 1 and 2 is the reverse of the temple building process in the ancient world."],[1298.4,"You build sort of a mountain, if you're building a ziggurat, you build a temple, pyramid, whatever."],[1304.41,"Usually shaped like a mountain, uh, like the mountain of God, which is where paradise is, and then um you build everything, you set everything in order, and then the last step is you go and you put an image, you put an idol of the god in there."],[1318.21,"And they did this ritual called the opening of the nostrils."],[1321.83,"Where they then do that, the spirit of God of the God then comes into the idol."],[1327.61,"And so now you're good to go to feed it and water it and clothe it and get it to do what you want."],[1333.83,"That's the idea."],[1335.27,"And this gets reversed and turned on its head in Genesis 1 and 2."],[1340.09,"Because it's God who comes and creates the garden."],[1344.61,"Paradise. And puts it on the mountain."],[1347.89,"And then he goes and makes an image of himself."],[1351.51,"He makes Adam as his own image and puts him in the garden."],[1356.87,"And then he breathes the breath of life into him."],[1359.77,"He breathes his spirit into him and brings him to life to serve as his image the way we were talking about."],[1367.95,"And so they understood this verse as being about Adam and about once that's done."],[1377.49,"Well, now, if he's the image of God, if he's serving as the image of God in creation now, then the angels, God's other creatures, his previous creatures, who are who, they need to come and bow down and serve human."],[1397.62,"Well, devil didn't like that much."],[1400.74,"And so you get this idea of envy"],[1401.18,"And you see this, the church fathers talk about this"],[1408.25,"You can look at St."],[1411.29,"Andrew of Caesarea in his commentary on Revelation chapter 12."],[1415.26,"He says, we must accept what the fathers teach that after the creation of the world the devil fell through envy."],[1421.9,"Pretty clear. Sixth century."],[1425.84,"Talking about this idea."],[1428.02,"So the devil saw this unique relationship between God and humanity, having to do with him as the image, and everything that implies, which includes what we call theosis."],[1439.8,"Includes what would become the incarnation."],[1442.68,"And that's why he ends up falling"],[1443.17,"And he's jealous of that."],[1448.13,"He can't, you know, we we talk about, we've got a lot of John Milton in our heads."],[1453.49,"Uh so there's this of it."],[1456.53,"We have this in our popular imaginary, there's this idea that the devil's like, I'm gonna have a revolution, and I'm gonna throw God off his throne."],[1466.29,"You know, Puritans were big on that kind of thing."],[1468.69,"Um, but right, um, but like, if you think about it for a few seconds, like, how would you do that?"],[1481.41,"Why would you think it could succeed?"],[1483.65,"Well, how would it even be possible?"],[1487.73,"Like, what lever could you use to remove God from right"],[1495.69,"Like, if you were I, I'm gonna make myself God."],[1500.97,"Okay, what's step one?"],[1502.83,"Like a creature can't."],[1506.89,"So that whole idea, even though it, you know, Milton's a fun read."],[1513.26,"Dog don't hunt."],[1515.79,"The idea is that, well, devil can't get it, God."],[1520.11,"He can't do anything to God."],[1522.2,"He's a creature."],[1523.8,"Uh but he could try to destroy his fellow creatures."],[1528.68,"He c he can do something to Adam and Eve"],[1534.06,"He can make them evil like himself."],[1537.92,"He can't try to corrupt them."],[1539.2,"He can try to corrupt God's creation."],[1541.04,"He can be sort of parasitic."],[1543.3,"And this is this is gonna be one of the major themes in Revelation twelve."],[1546.34,"That's why St."],[1547.38,"Andrew brings it up there is this idea that the the dragon there in Revelation twelve really wants to go after Christ, but can't really do anything to Christ, and so he goes after his followers."],[1558.02,"He goes after the people Christ loves."],[1562.26,"Same kind of idea."],[1563.46,"He can't go after God himself, but he can go after these humans who God loves so much, and that's why he's angry in the first place."],[1571.7,"And envious in the first place."],[1578.14,"So but again."],[1580.83,"He's saying that the son, capital S is different from the angelic sons of God."],[1587.55,"Why? Because he's human."],[1593.64,"Because he's human."],[1595.16,"He's not just the descendant of David."],[1597.96,"He is the uh descendant of Adam."],[1603.77,"So it's not just the Messianic king, but also the true man."],[1609.15,"The true human."],[1614.27,"Verse 7, and of the angels he says, Who makes his angels spirits, and his ministers a flame of fire."],[1627.35,"That's from another Psalm."],[1629.83,"Psalm 104 or 103."],[1633.99,"So he says that about the angels."],[1638.55,"He makes them spirits, his ministers a flame of fire."],[1642.79,"Verse 8 But to the Son, he says, Your throne, O God, is forever and ever."],[1649.27,"A scepter of righteousness is the scepter of your kingdom."],[1652.63,"You have loved righteousness and hated lawlessness."],[1655.51,"Therefore, God your God has anointed you with the oil of gladness more than your companions."],[1665.39,"And uh that is Psalm 45 or 44, 6 and 7."],[1673.55,"Um, and that psalm is talking about the Davidic King again."],[1682.77,"It's talking about the Davidic King."],[1685.73,"Meaning both the wine of David and the one particular."],[1690.31,"The Messiah, the son of David."],[1692.31,"Um, but notice your throne, O God, is forever and ever."],[1699.83,"A scepter of righteousness is the scepter of your kingdom."],[1703.43,"You have loved righteousness and hated laws, lawlessness, therefore, God your God has anointed you with the oil of gladness more than your companions."],[1712.51,"So the person being taught to is called God."],[1716.91,"But then there's God your God, the God of gods."],[1723.43,"Um, God, your God."],[1727.03,"Um, so were they saying David is God?"],[1732.31,"No. Or the rest of his descendants are gone, no."],[1736.17,"But uh, one of the things you find when you compare uh 2 Samuel 7, which is from where he just quoted uh the language about from the promise to David about his descendants, to the uh uh parallel passage in uh 1 Chronicles, um, is that in the version that you have in 2 Samuel, it says uh that it talks about a descendant of David sitting on uh David's throne and David's kingdom forever."],[1782.31,"It's your throne and your kingdom."],[1784.95,"In First Chronicles, it's a descendant of yours will sit on my throne in my kingdom forever."],[1794.53,"So you have this parallel where the throne of David becomes the throne of God."],[1801.97,"Those two thrones come together."],[1804.93,"Rather than just one being the icon."],[1807.67,"One being the image of the other."],[1809.68,"They become, they become the same."],[1812.88,"And that's reflected here in this psalm that's talking about the Davidic king."],[1818.38,"It says, Your throne, O God."],[1820.78,"It's the Davidic King, the ultimate king, the Messiah, is sitting on God's throne."],[1825.5,"First Chronicles. So, but the rest of the psalm requires."],[1833.93,"You have loved righteousness.ness and hated lawlessness, therefore, God your God has anointed you with the oil of glad more than your companions"],[1840.29,"That anointed with the oil, anointed with oil is how you were made king."],[1845.31,"Samuel anoints David."],[1850.35,"So there are our companions or fellows."],[1853.48,"There are human companions and uh and fellows involved."],[1860.88,"Um. So before we go on, notice again, we might think, like if we were sitting down to write this today."],[1876.66,"Like if if I was a really horrible Bible study teacher who wanted to run you all off, and so I said, we're gonna have essay tests."],[1886.82,"Um and I said, you know, write a couple page essay on the difference between the way Jesus Christ is the Son of God to the way angels are called sons of God."],[1903.68,"I think all of us, myself included, would start with, well, he's God himself."],[1909.98,"Angels are created, he's uncreated."],[1913.04,"That would be where I would start."],[1915.36,"And we're gonna get there."],[1916.72,"But that's in view two here in Hebrews."],[1919.04,"But that's not where Hebrew starts"],[1923.34,"Hebrews starts with the reason Jesus of Nazareth, the Christ, the Messiah, is greater than these angelic beings, is because he's the Christ or the Messiah."],[1937.18,"Is because he's also human."],[1945.66,"Meaning he has that special relationship that Adam had, but he fulfills it."],[1950.22,"He's the express image."],[1951.66,"He's not just called to be the image, he is the image."],[1955.94,"He did love righteousness and hate wickedness."],[1959.14,"And David, yeah."],[1964.27,"And he is the Davidic king."],[1967.33,"Who writes? He is the Messiah."],[1969.97,"That's point one for Hebrews that makes him greater than any of the angels."],[1976.69,"Because that is not true of any of the angelic beings."],[1980.29,"They may be vast cosmic intelligences, but that isn't true of them."],[1985.33,"They aren't that."],[1988.51,"And as he's slayed out here, humanity is itself in a certain way greater than the angelic creation."],[2000.83,"And so that by itself mak makeses him him greater."],[2005.23,"But then it continues"],[2013.59,"Verse 10. And you, Lord, in the beginning, laid the foundation of the earth, and the heavens are the work of your hands."],[2026.47,"They will perish, but you remain, and they will all grow old like a garment."],[2031.67,"Like a cloak you will fold them up, and they will be changed."],[2035.35,"But you are the same, and your years will not fail."],[2041.19,"Now notice that's joined with an and, to the quote, the quote we just had."],[2049.25,"Meaning these two. things are being put in parallel"],[2053.93,"So on one hand, we have Christ from among his fellows and his companions being anointed, being enthroned."],[2066.01,"This is parallel to something we saw him do in that first sentence."],[2069.77,"Now, on the other hand, we have him being eternal."],[2076.74,"Him being from forever."],[2080.6,"Everything's created again through Christ."],[2084.46,"And like that idea that St."],[2085.98,"Augustine picked up on of the seculum."],[2089.64,"Of the world."],[2091.42,"All this perishes, it grows old."],[2092.86,"Gets replaced by something new"],[2093.46,"It wears out."],[2097.27,"But not God."],[2100.23,"And not the God the Son as well."],[2103.45,"Same thing. So again, we why do we put this in parallel?"],[2109.29,"Well, we don't want you to go too far in the one direction."],[2113.13,"Oh, okay, he's greater because he's human."],[2115.37,"So the sun is human."],[2117.45,"Well, hold your horses."],[2120.07,"Yes. But also."],[2122.73,"Don't go too far down that trail."],[2124.41,"Come back a little this way."],[2128.46,"Verse 13. And he and here he flips over the card."],[2134.3,"But to which of the angels has he ever said, Sit at my right hand till I make your enemies your footstool."],[2142.02,"There it is."],[2142.74,"Psalm 10, 109, verse 1."],[2147.91,"Referring to that whole enthronement, Daniel 7, the whole thing."],[2151.27,"He didn't say that to any of the angels."],[2155.17,"He didn't say that to the angels."],[2156.53,"Only to the Son, through whom he created the ages, and who is human, who is the son of man, the son of Adam."],[2168.05,"Which is what that literally says in Hebrew."],[2170.05,"Benadam. Uh the son of Adam, uh, and who is who is enthroned, who is the Messianic king."],[2177.87,"This is the same person."],[2178.59,"Laid out here pretty clearly"],[2185.09,"Verse 14 Are they not all ministering spirits sent forth to minister for those who will inherit salvation?"],[2193.33,"Who's the they here?"],[2194.53,"The angels. He didn't say that to the angels."],[2198.87,"That's not the destiny of angels."],[2202.15,"That's the destiny of man."],[2204.88,"That's those who will inherit salvation."],[2208.18,"Humanity who inherits salvation are served by the angels."],[2212.82,"That Deuteronomy 32 quote."],[2215.56,"The angels are God's servants for aiding in the salvation of humanity."],[2221.08,"Christ is the son and heir"],[2222.82,"But we're not the singular heir."],[2224.74,"We're not the son and heir."],[2227.7,"But we're the ones who through him inherit salvation."],[2231.38,"But the angels are there to serve us and therefore to serve him."],[2236.98,"They don't accomplish salvation."],[2240.98,"They aren't the ones who bring salvation."],[2243.22,"They aren't the heirs of salvation."],[2248.74,"Yes, sir. This is a matter of we've going in one direction and I want to keep an eye on the case."],[2257.62,"Okay. We've talked about how man is superior to the angels."],[2263.22,"Why is it said that we're only done with? the angels"],[2267.82,"Well, I'm gonna punt on that."],[2271.66,"Because if you look, uh chapter two, a little ways into chapter two."],[2280.86,"It quotes that passage at length."],[2282.86,"So that's exactly where Hebrews is going."],[2285.26,"Okay. But that's gonna wait until next time."],[2290.54,"So we have a cliffhanger."],[2292.62,"Um yeah, we're gonna get into that in chapter two, because that's exactly where this homily is gonna go."],[2298.3,"We've got it in the one direction and now we're gonna come back in the other."],[2302.78,"Yes, sir. What was the Psalm quoted in verse 13?"],[2306.38,"Uh 13 is Psalm 11100 or 9 in the Greek."],[2309.98,"That's the one that the whole epistle is going to be"],[2312.5,"That's the one that's being taught."],[2316.42,"And if you go and read Psalm 1, I mean it's short, so I mean you can read it real quick."],[2320.42,"Psalm 110, 109 if you're looking up into the Orthodox Study Bible."],[2331.62,"He starts out with it here and then like it all plays out across the book."],[2339.06,"So, and and you know, we the way we're taught to preach now, uh, mainly by Protestants, um I'll be controversial."],[2350.1,"Uh having taken having take having taken preaching courses now uh uh at a from an Orthodox seminary."],[2355.14,"at And a good Orthodox seminary, uh we're just like cribbing from the Protestants on homiletics."],[2364.7,"Um like we would not diverge like this."],[2373.58,"Like if we were preaching on a psalm, like we would like we'd be like breaking down the grammar of the verses and like sticking pretty close to it and everything."],[2384.7,"And so it may seem like well, if he's talking about Psalm 110, why does he keep quoting all this other stuff?"],[2389.82,"And again, and another quote, and then, and this, and then, and then, and then."],[2394.91,"Uh, but uh all I could say is uh look at some of the Dead Sea Scrolls."],[2401.67,"This is just how they did it."],[2404.8,"This is just how they did it."],[2406.72,"It's you want to understand this verse, well, think about, and remember, because when you quote, you're not just quoting the one verse, you're quoting like the whole idea."],[2416.5,"The whole chunk."],[2418.42,"So it's okay, well, think about this passage, think about this passage, think about this passage."],[2426.15,"Now read this verse."],[2428.81,"That's sort of the way it's done."],[2431.59,"So we have this idea, this idea, this idea, that idea."],[2435.83,"Okay, now let's read the verse together."],[2438.55,"See how it connects."],[2440.44,"That's the approach."],[2441.32,"and And so so that that's's that was very common first century BC, first century AD what's going on here in Hebrews."],[2449.76,"That's why he keeps throwing in all these other passages."],[2452.9,"Because it's you've got to have these ideas in your head to be able to read and understand it."],[2458.74,"And this is just basically true for the way we interpret everything."],[2463.16,"It's not just learning the language."],[2465.1,"Like one of the one of the hardest things, uh I I read I read some Dostoevsky before I was Orthodox, and I read some Dostoevsky after I I you know entered the Orthodox Church."],[2478.62,"It made a lot more sense afterwards."],[2481.18,"Um, you know, because you get these weird things."],[2484.06,"My one of my favorites is there's an English translation of uh I think it's Brothers Karmazov, uh, where at one point a person's trying to ask someone if they're religious."],[2495.38,"And it's translated as, are you a pious man?"],[2498.1,"Do you eat pancakes for the dead?"],[2502.66,"Yeah, for your average American Protestant, it was like, What?"],[2507.46,"Pancakes for the dead."],[2508.82,"What kind of weird."],[2510.26,"Um and uh of course it was someone got confused by the word panakida, I think."],[2515.7,"Uh like but if you if you don't know, if you don't know Russian and Orthodox Christian customs, uh and manners and ways of dress and everything, and you try and read a Russian novel from the 19th century, unless it has like super extensive footnotes and annotations, you're gonna get confused."],[2538.97,"Um you have to have we call this being meta now."],[2544.99,"And you you do sub-references."],[2547.01,"But if if you have comedy or something where the joke is that they're quoting a line from a classic movie and you've never seen the movie, you're not gonna get the joke."],[2556.49,"So any any communication, any writing, any preaching, any teaching, in order to understand it, you have to, as best you can approximate what the original writer and hearers would have had in their head. in order to interpret it and understand it correctly"],[2575.99,"It's not just the language."],[2577.45,"It's all those things culturally and everything and ways of seeing."],[2582.33,"And so uh that's what lies behind this kind of mode of preaching."],[2593.55,"It's you're gonna look at this verse, you're gonna think it's weird."],[2597.33,"It's not gonna make any sense to you, or you're not gonna see how this has anything to do with Christ, or you're not gonna see how it has anything to do with the point I'm making, unless you are also remembering this part of scripture and that part of scripture, and this idea and that idea."],[2611.65,"And if you have all those in mind, then it'll all click."],[2615.41,"Oh, now it makes sense"],[2619.24,"And so that's that's what lies behind this method that's being used in in Hebrews."],[2625.8,"Um he didn't."],[2630.66,"I won't digress on this too much, but every once in a while, uh you get folks, I'll put it that way, you get folks who uh will say, Well, why are you using these modern terms?"],[2649.72,"Like, why are you looking at the modern scholarship and this and that and the other thing?"],[2653.64,"Like, ancient people didn't have that."],[2656.04,"People who wrote the Bible didn't have those terms."],[2658.2,"Like, St Paul didn't know what being meta was."],[2664.15,"Like these terms."],[2666.87,"Um, and what that misunderstands is that uh modern terms are terms that are used to convey concepts."],[2676.39,"And the fact that a term didn't exist doesn't mean the concept didn't exist."],[2682.01,"And so the concept and the understanding of what we today call intertextuality."],[2687.45,"That's the fancy."],[2689.41,"If you go and spend a lot of money like I did to get a biblical studies PhD, you learn about intertextuality, which is just like, hey, this text references that text."],[2701.77,"But intertextuality is the fancy word"],[2707.73,"For what this is."],[2708.87,"Taking this text and that text and this text and bringing them together and showing the relationship and drawing meaning out of the relationship."],[2718.07,"None of those terms existed, but that's what Hebrews is doing."],[2723.53,"That's very clearly."],[2724.65,"And we now have come up with this term to refer to that."],[2729.37,"Rather than just saying, hey, that thing Hebrews does."],[2732.97,"Or, you know when they like quote the one Bible verse and then they quote like another Bible verse, and then like they take a third path."],[2740.79,"It's much easier to just say like intertextuality, even though it's a modern term. to convey that when we communicate"],[2749.95,"Um so yeah, the fact that that you're using modern terms or some kind of modern method to try to get back at ancient ideas does not mean you're a modernist."],[2761.87,"That's not the same thing."],[2765.39,"Um but anyway."],[2768.91,"Thank you, everybody."],[2770.67,"And we'll get it pick up here and get into chapter two next week."],[2775.23,"Listen next time as Father Stephen DeYoung continues his study of the scriptures on the Whole Counsel of God."],[2782.51,"Father Stephen's email address is wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com."],[2787.87,"That's whole counsel at ancient faith.com"]],"o":[{"t":22.08,"lv":2,"h":"Hebrews 1:5 - Psalm 2, \"today I have begotten you\"","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Psalm 2</b> = used at <b>enthronement and crowning</b> of the king of Israel</li><li class=\"d1\">announcement: <b>\"Today you are my son\"</b></li><li class=\"d1\">OT king seen as <b>Son of God</b> in a certain sense</li><li class=\"d0\">This whole section = <b>disambiguating</b> \"son of God\" (\"nice modern internet term\")</li><li class=\"d1\">\"sons of God\" used many ways in OT</li><li class=\"d1\">one use: a <b>high rank of angels</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Christ as Son is <b>not</b> part of that angelic rank</li><li class=\"d0\">Yes the <b>Christ/Messiah</b> - but <b>more</b>: Son in a way <b>David wasn't</b></li><li class=\"d1\">David pointed to it, wasn't it himself</li></ul>"},{"t":74.68,"lv":3,"h":"Son of God: what the title means","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">King = son of God as <b>God's image on earth</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Adam</b> called Son of God in <b>Luke's genealogy</b> (\"pretty sure\" Luke, not Matthew) - made in God's image</li><li class=\"d0\">Image goes <b>beyond physical resemblance</b></li><li class=\"d1\">son learns <b>attributes, character traits</b> from father - nature and nurture</li><li class=\"d1\">an <b>ongoing relationship</b>, not just looks</li><li class=\"d0\">Son can <b>honor or dishonor</b> his father by how he lives</li></ul>"},{"t":237.11,"lv":3,"h":"Honor your father and mother","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Ten Commandments: <b>not</b> \"do whatever your parents tell you\" (how parents always read it)</li><li class=\"d0\">Means: live so as to <b>bring honor</b> to them</li><li class=\"d1\">if parents sin, may mean <b>not doing what they say</b> - \"being better\" than they taught</li><li class=\"d1\">people see it and say, as to Christ, <b>\"Blessed is the womb that bore you\"</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Relationship is <b>unbreakable</b></li><li class=\"d1\">even if father disowns son = <b>public scandal</b></li><li class=\"d1\">always there <i>de facto</i></li><li class=\"d0\">Big part of how <b>image of God</b> understood in OT</li></ul>"},{"t":307.44,"lv":3,"h":"King called to image God","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Adam made in image = <b>called to image God</b> in the world, not just resemble him</li><li class=\"d0\">King proclaimed Son of God because called to do the same</li><li class=\"d0\">King of Israel (later Judah) called to:</li><li class=\"d1\">bring <b>God's justice</b>, <b>compassion</b>, <b>leadership</b> into the world</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>teach the commandments</b>, guide the nation</li><li class=\"d1\">conduct himself to bring <b>honor to God</b>, never dishonor</li><li class=\"d0\">Used at coronation as <b>reminder: \"you are now God's son\"</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>statement of responsibility</b>, not \"you got adopted and he's got a really cool house\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Cited here to disambiguate from <b>angels</b> - none ever told this; \"certain humans have, angels haven't\"</li></ul>"},{"t":430.86,"lv":2,"h":"Hebrews 1:5b - 2 Samuel 7, \"I will be to him a father\"","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Quote from <b>2 Samuel</b> - God through <b>Nathan</b> to David about his descendant(s)</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Seed</b> = Heb. <i>zerah</i>, usually \"descendant(s)\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>collective noun</b> like deer, fish: \"one deer in my yard\" / \"twelve fish, one fish\"</li><li class=\"d1\">Hebrew <b>plays with it</b>, e.g. promises to <b>Abraham</b> - sometimes plural, sometimes singular</li><li class=\"d1\">same with David; they understood this → <b>looking for a Messiah</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Promise to David and <b>kings of Judah</b> after him</li><li class=\"d1\">and to <b>one particular descendant</b> - the <b>Messiah</b>, the anointed king</li><li class=\"d0\">Both quotes = <b>Davidic king</b> in general and the Messiah</li></ul>"},{"t":536.73,"lv":3,"h":"Human king, yet God","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">What makes Christ different from angelic sons: also a <b>human king</b> - no angel is</li><li class=\"d0\">Not an angel: <b>\"angels aren't humans\"</b></li><li class=\"d0\">But already told he's <b>God</b> (\"through him created the ages\")</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Divine and human</b> not invented in 2nd-5th century</li><li class=\"d1\">\"right here in the first, what, six verses of Hebrews\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Still inside frame of <b>Psalm 110 (109)</b>:1 - \"The Lord said to my Lord…\"</li><li class=\"d1\">Christ's question to scribes: if Messiah is David's son, why does David call him <b>Lord</b>?</li><li class=\"d1\">Hebrews is elaborating that: Messiah <b>not just human descendant</b></li></ul>"},{"t":664.83,"lv":2,"h":"Hebrews 1:6 - Deuteronomy 32, \"let all the angels worship him\"","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Quote from <b>Deuteronomy 32</b> - one of the <b>oldest pieces of OT</b> in unaltered form</li><li class=\"d1\">only rival in Torah: <b>Exodus 15</b>, Song of the Sea</li><li class=\"d1\">Hebrew almost impossible to translate - <b>Paleo-Hebrew</b>, \"dialect of old Canaanite\"</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Moses wrote the Torah\" not technically correct for Torah as we have it</li><li class=\"d1\">conservative scholars say <b>Mosaic origin</b>, not authorship</li><li class=\"d1\">language of oldest form <b>didn't exist</b> in Moses' day</li><li class=\"d0\">Moses wrote some stuff → translated, edited, <b>updated</b></li><li class=\"d1\">obvious updates: city named \"which used to be called…\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Shouldn't give \"<b>Hebie jeebies</b>\": Church accepted <b>Greek translation</b> (3rd c. BC) as equally authoritative</li><li class=\"d1\">\"nobody thinks Moses wrote in Greek\"</li></ul>"},{"t":901.57,"lv":3,"h":"Masoretic text, Dead Sea Scrolls, Septuagint","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"Original Hebrew\" = manuscripts from ~<b>11th c. AD</b> = <b>Masoretic Text (MT)</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Masoretes</b> = school of Jewish scribes</li><li class=\"d1\">added <b>vowel marks</b> - Hebrew written without vowels, like Arabic</li><li class=\"d1\">vowels change meaning → marking = <b>interpretation</b>; remove them, read differently</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>margin notes</b> all around the Hebrew Bible</li><li class=\"d1\">saw a mistake? <b>copied it anyway</b>, noted in margin \"we think this should say…\"</li><li class=\"d1\">contrast: Christian NT copyists \"constantly trying to fix things\" - but <b>thousands of manuscripts</b> let us correct</li><li class=\"d0\">Newer Bibles in Deut 32 use <b>Dead Sea Scrolls</b>, <b>4QDeut</b> (cave 4, Qumran)</li><li class=\"d1\">nobody tried to fix it; matches the <b>Greek</b> better</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Septuagint</b> translators had same unclear Paleo-Hebrew, interpreted it</li><li class=\"d1\">DSS Hebrew 1000+ years older: \"oh, that's why they did that\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Some translations just <b>throw up hands and translate the Greek</b> - Hebrews' quote follows the Greek</li><li class=\"d0\">Try 12 translations on <b>BibleGateway/Bible Hub</b> - they differ</li></ul>"},{"t":1164.67,"lv":2,"h":"The firstborn is Adam","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Who is the <b>firstborn</b> brought into the world? <b>Adam</b></li><li class=\"d0\">\"Let all the angels of God worship him\" - \"wait, what?\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>worship</b> used loosely = <b>bow before</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Elaborated with <b>the fall of the devil</b> in <b>Second Temple literature</b> (6th c. BC - 1st c. AD)</li><li class=\"d1\">written to answer questions, connect dots</li><li class=\"d1\">question: why does the devil, seen falling in <b>Genesis 3</b>, go after Adam and Eve and get cursed?</li><li class=\"d1\">idea even picked up in <b>Islam</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1285.02,"lv":3,"h":"Temple-building reversed in Genesis 1-2","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Man made in image of God</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Ancient temple building</b>: build a mountain-shaped ziggurat/pyramid (mountain of God = paradise), set everything in order</li><li class=\"d1\">last step: put the <b>image/idol</b> of the god in</li><li class=\"d1\">ritual = <b>opening of the nostrils</b> → spirit of the god enters idol</li><li class=\"d1\">now feed, water, clothe it</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Genesis 1-2 turns it on its head</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>God</b> creates garden/paradise on the mountain</li><li class=\"d1\">then makes an <b>image of himself</b> - Adam - puts him there</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>breathes breath of life</b> into him</li><li class=\"d0\">They read this verse as about Adam: if he is God's image, angels (God's <b>previous creatures</b>) must <b>bow and serve</b> human</li></ul>"},{"t":1397.62,"lv":3,"h":"Devil's fall through envy","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Devil didn't like that</b> → <b>envy</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>St. Andrew of Caesarea</b>, commentary on <b>Revelation 12</b>: \"after the creation of the world the devil fell through <b>envy</b>\" - 6th century</li><li class=\"d0\">Devil saw unique relationship of God and humanity</li><li class=\"d1\">man as image, includes <b>theosis</b></li><li class=\"d1\">includes what would become the <b>incarnation</b></li><li class=\"d1\">jealous → falls</li><li class=\"d0\">Popular imaginary = <b>John Milton</b>: revolution, throw God off his throne</li><li class=\"d1\">\"Puritans were big on that\"</li><li class=\"d1\">think for a few seconds: <b>what lever</b> could a creature use? \"<b>Dog don't hunt</b>\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1523.8,"lv":3,"h":"Parasitic against creation","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Can't touch God - he's a creature</li><li class=\"d0\">Can go after <b>fellow creatures</b>: corrupt Adam and Eve, \"make them evil like himself\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>parasitic</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Major theme of <b>Revelation 12</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>dragon</b> wants Christ, can't touch him → goes after <b>his followers</b>, \"the people Christ loves\"</li><li class=\"d1\">same pattern: angry and envious because God loves humans so much</li></ul>"},{"t":1580.83,"lv":2,"h":"The Son as true man","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Son (capital S) different from angelic sons <b>because he's human</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not just descendant of <b>David</b> but of <b>Adam</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not just <b>Messianic king</b> but the <b>true man</b>, the true human</li></ul>"},{"t":1614.27,"lv":2,"h":"Hebrews 1:7-9 - angels vs. the Son's throne","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>1:7</b> angels: \"makes his angels spirits, his ministers a flame of fire\" - <b>Psalm 104 (103)</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>1:8-9</b> to the Son: \"Your throne, O God, is forever and ever…\" - <b>Psalm 45 (44):6-7</b></li><li class=\"d1\">psalm about the <b>Davidic king</b> - David's line and the <b>Messiah</b></li><li class=\"d1\">addressee <b>called God</b>, yet \"God, your God\" - \"God of gods\"</li><li class=\"d1\">David is God? <b>No</b> - nor his descendants</li><li class=\"d0\">Compare <b>2 Samuel 7</b> with parallel <b>1 Chronicles</b></li><li class=\"d1\">2 Sam: descendant on <b>David's throne</b>, <b>your</b> kingdom</li><li class=\"d1\">1 Chron: descendant on <b>my throne</b> in <b>my kingdom</b> forever</li><li class=\"d0\">→ <b>throne of David becomes throne of God</b> - not one icon of the other, they <b>become the same</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Anointed with oil = how you're made king (<b>Samuel anoints David</b>)</li><li class=\"d1\">\"more than your companions\" → <b>human companions and fellows</b> involved</li></ul>"},{"t":1860.88,"lv":2,"h":"What Hebrews leads with","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Imagine an <b>essay test</b>: how is Christ Son of God differently from angels?</li><li class=\"d1\">we'd all start: he's <b>God, uncreated</b>; angels created</li><li class=\"d1\">that comes (view two) - <b>but not where Hebrews starts</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Hebrews starts: greater than angels because he's the <b>Christ/Messiah</b> and <b>also human</b></li><li class=\"d1\">the special relationship <b>Adam</b> had - he <b>fulfills</b> it</li><li class=\"d1\">not just called to be the image - <b>he is the image</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>loved righteousness, hated wickedness</b>; is the Davidic king</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Point one</b> making him greater than any angel</li><li class=\"d1\">\"vast cosmic intelligences\" - not true of them</li><li class=\"d0\">Humanity itself in a way <b>greater than the angelic creation</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2013.59,"lv":2,"h":"Hebrews 1:10-12 - Lord who laid the foundation","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Quote joined by <b>\"and\"</b> → two things <b>in parallel</b></li><li class=\"d1\">one: Christ among companions <b>anointed, enthroned</b></li><li class=\"d1\">other: Christ <b>eternal</b>, from forever; all created through him</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>\"They will perish, but you remain\"</b> - world grows old like a garment, folded up, changed</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Augustine</b>'s idea of the <i>seculum</i> = the world</li><li class=\"d1\">not God, nor God the Son: \"you are the same\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Why parallel? Don't go too far one way: <b>\"he's greater because he's human\"</b> - \"hold your horses\" - come back this way</li></ul>"},{"t":2128.46,"lv":2,"h":"Hebrews 1:13-14 - sit at my right hand; ministering spirits","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>1:13</b> \"flips over the card\": to which angel did he say <b>\"Sit at my right hand…\"</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Psalm 110 (109):1</b> - the whole enthronement, <b>Daniel 7</b></li><li class=\"d1\">said only to the Son: creator of the ages <b>and</b> human, <b>son of man</b> = <i>ben-adam</i>, \"son of Adam\" in Hebrew</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>same person</b>, laid out clearly</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>1:14</b> angels = <b>ministering spirits</b> sent for those who <b>inherit salvation</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not the destiny of angels - the destiny of <b>man</b></li><li class=\"d1\">ties to <b>Deut 32</b> quote: angels serve God in aiding human salvation</li><li class=\"d1\">Christ = <b>son and heir</b>; we aren't the singular heir but inherit through him</li><li class=\"d1\">angels serve us, therefore serve him; they <b>don't accomplish or bring salvation</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2248.74,"lv":2,"h":"Q&amp;A: man and the angels","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Q: we've said <b>man is superior to angels</b> - why is it said (inaudible) only…?</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>punt</b>: chapter 2 <b>quotes that passage at length</b></li><li class=\"d1\">exactly where Hebrews is going - \"cliffhanger\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Homily goes <b>one direction</b> first, then <b>comes back the other</b> in chapter 2</li><li class=\"d0\">Q: what Psalm quoted in <b>v.13</b>?</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Psalm 110 / 109</b> in Greek (Orthodox Study Bible numbering)</li><li class=\"d1\">the one the <b>whole epistle</b> is teaching - starts here, plays out across the book</li><li class=\"d1\">short; read it</li></ul>"},{"t":2339.06,"lv":2,"h":"Why so many quotations: how they preached","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Modern preaching learned \"mainly from <b>Protestants</b>\" - even good Orthodox seminary \"cribbing\" on homiletics</li><li class=\"d1\">stick close to the verses, break down grammar</li><li class=\"d1\">so: if preaching on Psalm 110, why all the other quotes?</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Dead Sea Scrolls</b>: \"this is just how they did it\"</li><li class=\"d1\">quoting a verse = quoting the <b>whole idea, whole chunk</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"think about this passage… and this… now read this verse\" → <b>see how it connects</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Common 1st c. BC / 1st c. AD approach</li><li class=\"d1\">need those ideas <b>in your head</b> to understand it</li></ul>"},{"t":2458.74,"lv":3,"h":"Needing the context: Dostoevsky","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">True of all interpretation - not just learning the language</li><li class=\"d0\">Read <b>Dostoevsky</b> before and after becoming Orthodox - \"made a lot more sense afterwards\"</li><li class=\"d1\">English translation (<i>Brothers Karamazov</i>): \"Are you a pious man? <b>Do you eat pancakes for the dead?</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">Protestant: \"what?\" - confusion over <i>panikhida</i></li><li class=\"d1\">need Russian and <b>Orthodox customs, manners, dress</b> or heavy footnotes</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Being \"meta\"</b>: joke quoting a classic movie you never saw</li><li class=\"d0\">Any writing/preaching: approximate what <b>original writer and hearers had in their head</b></li><li class=\"d1\">culture, ways of seeing - not just language</li><li class=\"d0\">Hebrews' method: this verse looks weird until you remember <b>this and that passage</b> → \"<b>oh, now it makes sense</b>\"</li></ul>"},{"t":2630.66,"lv":2,"h":"Modern terms: intertextuality","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Objection: why use <b>modern terms</b>, modern scholarship? \"St. Paul didn't know what being <b>meta</b> was\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>terms convey concepts</b>; term not existing ≠ concept not existing</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Intertextuality</b> = \"this text references that text\" - learned in a <b>biblical studies PhD</b></li><li class=\"d1\">taking this text and that text, <b>showing the relationship</b>, drawing out meaning</li><li class=\"d1\">Hebrews does exactly that - no such term then</li><li class=\"d1\">easier than \"that thing Hebrews does\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Using modern terms/methods to get back at <b>ancient ideas</b> ≠ being a <b>modernist</b></li></ul>"}],"next":"Next week: <b>Hebrews 2</b>"});
